december 7th forever the day that lives  in infamy and that's because 80 years  ago today more than 2 000 americans were  killed during japan's attack on pearl  harbor in hawaii it led to the u.s  declaring war on japan entering world  war ii and eventually leading the allies  to victory  tom chakansky is senior curator at the  national world war ii museum in new  orleans and he joins us now to talk  about the importance of pearl harbor  remembrance day  tom welcome to quick take thank you so  much for taking the time to speak with  us on what's been a very busy day for  you  um for a lot of people  9 11 has actually eclipsed december 7th  as the day of infamy especially those  who are young  how do you explain pearl harbor day to  those who only remember a post a 911  world  well pearl harbor really is  very much equivalent  to 9 11.  and of course the the biggest thing is  that although  911's triggered a long long war  the pearl harbor attack  brought the united states into a world  war  and one that we won as opposed to what's  happened after 9 11 where we're still  kind of sifting through  the fallout from everything  tell us what your museum the national  world war ii museum in new orleans is  doing to remember the day i understand  you have an exhibit on pearl harbor and  it's going to be around for a while  yes we've just recently opened infamy  pearl harbor remembered and that's a new  special exhibit that looks at how pearl  harbor is remembered and that'll be open  through june but of course in our  permanent galleries we have another more  extensive exhibit that examines the road  to war in pearl harbor and the actions  of that day and we also include pearl  harbor in our signature film beyond all  boundaries which gives a 45-minute  overview of the war so  a very important uh event for america in  world war ii  and of course we're still in the middle  of a pandemic so i wonder how  the current situation covet protocol  plays a role in your planning for  the exhibit and and how you're  commemorating the anniversary  well we are fortunate enough that given  the current situation we've been able to  have live events today but given our  experience over the last two years we  also continue to have virtual  virtual uh presentations  because it does reach so many more  people  yeah you i mean it makes it accessible  to everyone in the nation and the world  really i was thinking back to how when i  was growing up i remember seeing world  war ii veterans uh going to pearl harbor  to commemorate december 7th speaking and  each year the ranks would get thinner  and thinner which of course makes sense  that doesn't really happen anymore  because most of them are no longer with  us and this week alone for instance we  lost uh the last surviving member of the  band of brothers edward shames and  former senator bob dole another world  war ii hero  do we know how many surviving world war  ii veterans there are left  i believe it's under 600 000  and that's of 16 million that served in  world war ii  and you were able to recruit uh one of  those veterans for your exhibit as well  because they'll be speaking he'll be  speaking to  uh your audience and and  and kind of commemorating the day for  your audience  yes at 11 o'clock we had a presentation  that included the last  uh  server the last survivor of pearl harbor  from louisiana  as far as we know  that's pretty impressive and i'm sure a  lot of people were looking forward to  his remarks and what he had to say  without  so many living survivors of world war ii  who are able to  share their stories firsthand we really  rely more than ever  on manufactured depictions of the war  hollywood depictions really we're  talking about tv shows and movies as  well so if you could talk a little bit  about how the band of brothers tv shows  like that and movies shape our  understanding of what happened  well at first i'd like to say that we're  very fortunate because over the last 20  years that the museum has been here  we've been collecting the personal  stories of those veterans and so they  may be gone today but we have recorded  their comments and their remembrances  and we're able to include those we have  15 individuals i don't think any of them  are along with us anymore whose stories  and remembrance of the day at pearl  harbor are included in our exhibit on  video but of course for many people  the movies about world war ii do play  a great uh  point in their understanding of the war  and it also helps that  from time to time these movies come  forth and they reinvigorate people's  interest in the war uh and it's not  unusual when you consider that many  films were made in the 70s and those  similar topics have been remade today  and it again  gets people interested and they  want to find out what their ancestors  did in world war ii and what happened in  more detail  what book or movie would you recommend  to young people who are  i mean obviously know quite a bit about  world war ii but want to really dig in  and learn more about what happened what  what's what's a personal recommendation  that you would put forth  well i think  dr ambrose band of brothers is a very  good book because it covers one group of  individuals  from the time they get into the service  until the end of world war ii and of  course they're just one group but  they're a great many uh groups and  that's a good place to start and then  after that i would recommend you find  something about an ancestor perhaps that  you had uh almost every unit's got a  unit history  find that and learn about what your  people did that's a great great piece of  advice i mean finding that personal  connection will always bring people  closer to the event at hand what  surprises you the most as visitors go  through the national world war ii museum  on pearl harbor remembrance day as as  they comment on the exhibit and on uh  the state of the world and then how the  country has responded to these moments  of challenges in the past  oh uh you know one of the things that  that most surprised me i had a dinner  with a curator last week and and we  often have people who talk about uh that  they were  at pearl harbor and we didn't have  anybody there who was at pearl harbor we  had a very original fellow he said you  know i'm the youngest survivor of pearl  harbor my mother was four months  pregnant with me when pearl harbor was  bombed she was there and i thought well  that that we've gotten younger and  younger with our veterans and survivors  and that one has probably uh topped the  list  it's good to know you'll you'll be able  to draw upon his expertise later on  if nothing else i want to bring it  forward to today because defense  secretary lloyd austin today released a  statement honoring the 80th anniversary  of pearl harbor day and he linked it  with the us's current efforts to protect  the indo-pacific region how would you  explain that link to your museum's  visitors who come through and are trying  to understand  how the past connects with what we're  seeing today  well after world war ii we made a  commitment to  the people in the pacific that we would  defend  their rights and  their countries and we made a  constitution  with japan that said they would only  have a self-defense force and so  we have a long-standing obligation to  protect these peoples uh we can no  longer afford as america wanted to do  before work before pearl harbor to say  it's a foreign war it's  uh happening elsewhere in the world  there no longer is an elsewhere in the  world we're all one world together and  we must act that way  well said tom jakansky really appreciate  your joining us today on this 80th  anniversary of the attack on pearl  harbor tom is senior curator at the  national world war ii museum in new  orleans thank you so much
 

